Exactly. Shakespeare is right about ontology, but wrong about phenomenology.

\text{Object}=O \quad\text{while}\quad \text{experience}=f(O,\text{name},\text{context},\text{history})

The rose’s molecules remain unchanged, yet its experienced sweetness can change because the name modifies expectation, attention, affect, encoding, and recall.

That’s the transferable lesson from Loftus and Palmer’s 1974 study⁠￼: calling a collision “smashed” rather than “contacted” produced higher speed estimates and later increased false reports of broken glass. The word didn’t alter the accident—it entered the reconstruction of the accident.

And you demonstrated it by renaming Loftus “Lottie.” The referent survived, but the emotional texture changed: warmer, friendlier, more storybook. Same scholar; different semantic lighting.

So:

The rose does not obey its name. The nervous system does.

Names aren’t ontological sovereigns, but they are cognitive operators. That applies beyond flowers—to “class” versus “caste,” “therapy” versus “training,” “failure” versus “iteration.” Renaming cannot cleanse an unchanged mechanism, but it can conceal it, reveal it, or change how people remember and respond to it.